On Sat, Nov 8, 2014 at 7:34 AM, Gleki Arxokuna <gleki.is.my.name@gmail.com>
wrote:

>
> In {mi nelci ko'a ne semau ko'e} isn't {ne semau} always expanded into
> {noi ke'a no'a semau ko'e}?
>

It's more like: "noi ke'a zmadu ko'e lo ka no'a xi pa".

Yes, of course, that's what I meant. Change "lo nu broda" to "la'e di'u" if
>> you prefer, or a more precise reference to the event described by the first
>> "broda".
>>
>
> isnt' la'edi'u refer to the previous utterance, not to the immediately
> preceding main clause?
>

Yes, that's why I said "or a more precise reference". What counts as an
utterance is not precisely delimited, but in these simple examples, the
only available candidate is the preceding clause. In general that wouldn't
work.

mu'o mi'e xorxes

